//! THE WALL, library-wide family: the `sqlite3_*` entry points that
//! reach the library itself rather than a connection — version,
//! compile options, initialization, memory, and the SQL-text
//! utilities. Every one is declared by its C name and exported, so a
//! caller who needs the wall reaches it with the value they hold.
//!
//! The signatures are checked BY HAND against `vendor/sqlite3.h`:
//! Avra does not read C headers, so the `extern fn` line is the
//! contract and the vendored header is what keeps it honest.
//!
//! THE FOUR DECLARATIONS THAT ANSWER `string`, AND WHY EACH IS ALLOWED
//! TO. The wall's rule is that a borrowed C pointer crosses as `ptr` and
//! the face copies, because the runtime cannot see a foreign pointer at
//! all — `hdr` refuses one without its tag, so retain and release no-op
//! and `AVRA_RC_GUARD` prints clean for a correct borrow and a
//! use-after-free alike. `string` is permitted ONLY where the text is
//! IMMORTAL, and each of the four was read in the amalgamation rather
//! than assumed:
//!
//! ```
//! sqlite3_libversion        -> sqlite3_version, a global const char[]   :187469
//! sqlite3_sourceid          -> the SQLITE_SOURCE_ID literal            :187477
//! sqlite3_compileoption_get -> sqlite3azCompileOpt[N], a
//!                              `static const char * const` table        :23088
//! sqlite3_errstr            -> aMsg[], a `static const char* const`
//!                              table of literals                       :189031
//! ```
//!
//! A FIFTH IS NOT ADDED BY ANALOGY. It is added by reading the body and
//! quoting the line, and the adversarial suite measures that the text
//! survives intervening calls rather than trusting that it will.
//!
//! AND THE RULE IS PROVISIONAL, not settled: `immortal` is a property of
//! the CALLEE that a declaration cannot enforce, so the day the language
//! can copy foreign text into a box these four become `ptr` plus a copy
//! like every other borrowed answer. Today it cannot — nothing in the
//! tree turns a `ptr` into a `string`, because every text-answering C
//! body we own copies inside itself (`str_static`, `str_owned`) and we
//! do not own SQLite's. Declaring these `ptr` now would not make them
//! safer; it would make them unreadable.
//!
//! A C ANSWER THAT CAN BE NULL IS DECLARED `string?`. Avra's niche
//! for an absent pointer IS the null pointer, so the two conventions
//! coincide; declared `string`, a NULL answer reaches `.length` and
//! the process dies in `strlen`.
//!
//! A C `int` IS 32 BITS AND AVRA'S IS 64, so a NEGATIVE C answer
//! reads here as a large positive one — the wall carries the C
//! truth, which is 32 bits wide. Result codes, counts and 0/1
//! verdicts are unharmed; the comparisons are not, and each says so
//! at its own declaration. The face asks those only for equality.

/// The library's version — "3.53.4". Never NULL.
export extern fn sqlite3_libversion() -> string

/// The version as `X*1000000 + Y*1000 + Z` — 3053004 for 3.53.4.
/// NOT the encoding the download filename uses.
export extern fn sqlite3_libversion_number() -> int

/// The check-in this build came from. Never NULL.
export extern fn sqlite3_sourceid() -> string

/// Whether the library was compiled with a given option, named
/// without its `SQLITE_` prefix.
export extern fn sqlite3_compileoption_used(name: string) -> int

/// The Nth compile option, and NULL once N is past the last.
export extern fn sqlite3_compileoption_get(n: int) -> string?

/// Zero only when the mutexing code was omitted.
export extern fn sqlite3_threadsafe() -> int

/// The library brought up. Idempotent; the library also calls it
/// itself unless compiled with SQLITE_OMIT_AUTOINIT, which this one
/// is not.
export extern fn sqlite3_initialize() -> int

/// The library taken down. Every connection must be closed first.
export extern fn sqlite3_shutdown() -> int

/// Whether the text ends in a complete, terminated SQL statement.
export extern fn sqlite3_complete(sql: string) -> int

/// English text for a primary or extended result code. Never NULL.
export extern fn sqlite3_errstr(code: int) -> string

/// How many keywords this build recognises.
export extern fn sqlite3_keyword_count() -> int

/// Whether the first `n` bytes of the word are a keyword.
export extern fn sqlite3_keyword_check(word: string, n: int) -> int

/// The LIKE operator's answer, with an escape character's code point
/// (zero for none). Zero when the pattern matches.
export extern fn sqlite3_strlike(pattern: string, text: string, escape: int) -> int

/// The GLOB operator's answer. Zero when the pattern matches.
export extern fn sqlite3_strglob(pattern: string, text: string) -> int

/// NARROW: C answers a signed 32-bit int, so a NEGATIVE result
/// reads here as a large positive one. Ask it for equality only
/// until the extern wall carries sized types.
/// ASCII-case-insensitive comparison, as SQLite's own collation does.
export extern fn sqlite3_stricmp(a: string, b: string) -> int

/// NARROW: C answers a signed 32-bit int, so a NEGATIVE result
/// reads here as a large positive one. Ask it for equality only
/// until the extern wall carries sized types.
/// The same over the first `n` bytes.
export extern fn sqlite3_strnicmp(a: string, b: string, n: int) -> int

/// Bytes of memory the library currently holds. Zero under
/// SQLITE_DEFAULT_MEMSTATUS=0 unless a connection turned it on.
export extern fn sqlite3_memory_used() -> int

/// The high-water mark, reset when asked.
export extern fn sqlite3_memory_highwater(reset: int) -> int

/// Memory the library allocated, given back. A NULL is a no-op. This
/// is what an OWNED answer from the wall goes back through —
/// `sqlite3_expanded_sql`'s text, and `sqlite3_exec`'s error message.
export extern fn sqlite3_free(p: ptr?)

/// Memory handed back to the operating system, in bytes.
export extern fn sqlite3_release_memory(bytes: int) -> int

/// The soft heap limit in bytes; answers the previous limit.
export extern fn sqlite3_soft_heap_limit64(bytes: int) -> int

/// The hard heap limit in bytes; answers the previous limit.
export extern fn sqlite3_hard_heap_limit64(bytes: int) -> int

/// Milliseconds slept, which the OS may shorten.
export extern fn sqlite3_sleep(ms: int) -> int

/// Shared-cache mode, deprecated by SQLite and kept bindable.
export extern fn sqlite3_enable_shared_cache(on: int) -> int

// NOT DECLARED, and the omission is a decision rather than an
// oversight: `sqlite3_global_recover`, `sqlite3_soft_heap_limit` and
// `sqlite3_thread_cleanup` carry SQLITE_DEPRECATED in the header and
// vanish the day the build takes SQLITE_OMIT_DEPRECATED;
// `sqlite3_activate_cerod` activates a proprietary extension this
// build does not carry. `sqlite3_os_init`/`sqlite3_os_end` belong to
// a custom VFS and are declared with that family.
